menu "Player configuration"

config PLAYER_MP3
    bool "Support MP3 playback (dial-code clips, prompts)"
    default n
    help
        Lets player_play() stream-decode MP3 files (vendors minimp3, same
        decoder audio_convert's upload-time CONFIG_AUDIO_CONVERT_MP3 option
        uses -- turning either one on compiles it in) instead of requiring
        everything to be raw PCM WAV. The point is storage: a full-length
        song at a low mono bitrate (e.g. 32kbps) is 3-4x smaller than the
        same audio as 8kHz/16-bit PCM WAV, which matters on a device with a
        few MB of flash for all its clips/recordings combined. See
        firmware/bringup/gen_prompt.py's --name *.mp3 handling for the
        host-side encode half of this.

        Deliberately a SEPARATE option from CONFIG_AUDIO_CONVERT_MP3 rather
        than reusing it, even though both compile the same decoder: that
        option's RAM cost is a PERMANENT one (its own Kconfig help explains
        why -- the httpd worker task, which runs for the device's entire
        uptime, needs a much bigger stack to survive minimp3's ~16KB decode
        local). This option's cost is instead paid only by player.c's own
        task, and only while something is actually playing (its stack is
        freed again afterward) -- a real but much cheaper and temporary
        tradeoff, not something you pay for just by having WiFi up. Turning
        this on with the upload option OFF gets MP3 PLAYBACK without also
        paying for MP3 BROWSER UPLOADS you may not need (gen_prompt.py's
        host-side pipeline uploads pre-encoded files through the plain
        byte-for-byte /media/<name> endpoint either way, upload option or
        not).

endmenu
